            "title": "Variables & Data Types",
            "pages": [
                {
                    "content": "Python variables don't need a declared type. Create one with a name, an equals sign, and a value. Python works out the type automatically, so there is no int, var, or let in front.",
                    "code": [],
                    "examples": [
                        "score = 10",
                        "name = \"Ava\"",
                        "price = 19.99"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "Common types are int for whole numbers, float for decimal numbers, str for text, and bool for True or False values.",
                    "code": [
                        "int",
                        "float",
                        "str",
                        "bool"
                    ],
                    "examples": [
                        "score = 0         # int",
                        "price = 19.99    # float",
                        "name = \"Ava\"     # str",
                        "is_active = True # bool"
                    ]
                },
                {
                    "content": "Text goes inside quotes to make a string. Booleans are written True and False with a capital first letter; lowercase true is not valid Python. A quoted \"True\" is text, not a Boolean.",
                    "code": [],
                    "examples": [
                        "is_active = True",
                        "is_admin = False",
                        "text = \"True\"  # str"
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "quiz": [
